& [JustificationIdentity] is Phase 2 activation reasoning
  + [justification_uuid] is a61ed9c9-8ebc-4b42-8d07-553af81ccd62
  + [solution_uuid] is 6f4a7123-b8ca-4d44-9b26-e64762a4fa88
  + [status] is implementation_justified

& [Need] is replace one mock boundary with observed live inference
  + [reason] is Phase 1 proves the ecosystem protocol but cannot prove that a current Codex model receives a work packet, invokes Cantor, and returns an admissible candidate
  + [dependency] is every Phase 1 authority, causation, replay, budget, review, and outcome gate passed

& [SelectedInterface] is stable app-server plus stable MCP
  + [app_server] provides official thread, turn, event, sandbox, approval, and completion control
  + [MCP] already provides the exact verified one-tool Cantor projection
  + [Rust_wire_client] preserves the project implementation language without adding a Node or Python runtime dependency
  + [dynamic_tools] are rejected for this slice because official documentation marks them experimental and stable MCP already satisfies the requirement

& [SmallestProof] is one ephemeral read-only turn
  + [advantages] is no workspace mutation, no persistent thread, no approval, no steering, one tool, one final answer, and direct reuse of the deterministic Observer
  + [measurement] is exact process and schema versions, event counts and ordering, tool and response digests, turn latency, forbidden action count, decision, and cleanup
  + [rollback] is remove or disable the live adapter while the entire mock and core path remains complete

& [AlternativeAssessment] is explicit
  + [Codex_SDK] is official and simpler for TypeScript or Python clients but adds a language runtime boundary and does not remove the need for ecosystem event validation
  + [codex_exec] is appropriate for isolated automation but exposes less structured bidirectional lifecycle evidence than app-server
  + [experimental_dynamic_tools] would remove MCP configuration but adds experimental API coupling and duplicate semantic projection
  + [direct_Responses_API] would build a different agent rather than verify the intended Codex control surface

& [FailureCost] is bounded
  + [live_nondeterminism] is contained by deterministic transport tests and one explicit opt-in proof rather than mandatory networked global tests
  + [interface_drift] is visible through exact version and generated schema evidence plus strict message parsing
  + [model_misbehavior] is a typed forbidden-event, missing-tool, malformed-candidate, or Observer rejection
  + [credential_risk] is minimized because Cantor delegates authentication to Codex and never reads its credential
  + [process_risk] is bounded by exact executable digests, child handles, timeouts, output limits, and cleanup

& [Decision] is implement RLC-001 through RLC-012
  + [phase] is one live read-only turn only
  + [later] is preserve writes, persistent sessions, manager iteration, steering, retry, approval forwarding, dynamic tools, notifications, multi-worker, and effects as new SJS work
  + [reconciliation] is renew authority for official wire corrections, strict-schema subset handling, supervisor-owned proof references, exact final-message binding, and real fail-closed probe observations
  + [hardening] is renew authority again for bounded producer flow, complete stderr drain, partial-spawn cleanup, returned-authority verification, strict startup notification admission, graceful exit proof, and unchanged live behavior
